{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks to determine both when a bounded run 'may later be resumed without corrupting the append-only identity of the recorded events' AND 'when resumption must stay refused.' SRC_001 evades the core question by redefining resumption as a new derived branch (a new run), not a continuation of the same run identity. The countercondition itself concedes this fails if 'resume' means 'continuing the exact same run identity without branching' — which is precisely what the problem is asking about. By asserting resumption is 'always possible' (as a branch), it never identifies any condition under which resumption must stay refused, leaving half the problem unaddressed.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 claims resumption is 'always safe at any point of exhaustion' under a purely functional model, but the problem explicitly requires determining 'when resumption must stay refused.' The artifact's claim provides no positive refusal criterion — it only notes counterconditions (global-state mutation, irreversible side effects) under which its own claim would be falsified, without translating those into a concrete adjudication of when to refuse. The problem asks to 'adjudicate between rival criteria wherever the evidence allows'; asserting universal safety under idealized assumptions does not adjudicate, it sidesteps the refusal question entirely.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}